        }            
    }       
}

Now, you can run this Pex file in Visual studio with PexLab, to check whether contracts are met.

There are limitations in PEXLab as of now (11/Sept/13), It doesn't handle the case where it doesn't meet the contract and goes in a loop.

You can always work on this, the team might release an update soon with some useful bug fixes and updates.

The contracts are pretty extensive and cover a lot of corner cases and use cases, as well as invalid inputs.

**Note: Always install the Pex and Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.Pex.Core NuGet packages before executing PEX files.**
Remember, Pex does not guarantee to find all of the "interesting" properties as it depends on the randomly generated tests but it will find the corner cases, invalid/boundary tests and any contract violation.

Kind regards,
Shreyas

P.S: This is just a basic overview. Contract based unit testing in Pex is much more than this. Please do check out the documentation for all the details.
     P.P.S:
This post is an overview only. You can find full details on this in "Microsoft Code Contracts Whitepaper" and the Pex documentation.
     P.P.P.S: 
The Pex tool was used as part of Microsoft Research's industrial-strength theorem proving tool SMT Interpolator project. It's a novel technique of automated test case generation. However, it was discontinued during research phase but is in use by multiple companies and it has been recently revived by Microsoft for their products. 

Microsoft Pex is a static analysis tool which automatically generates a set of test cases based on a specification. Pex reads the specification to understand the behavior of your functions and then generates a variety of different test cases that it feeds back to a static verification tool. 

This tool has gained a lot of attention lately because it helps developers to find a large number of bugs that would have been difficult to find by a casual or manual testing. It does this by creating a large number of tests that exercise a wide range of possible program states.
   
Please remember that this tool is meant to increase a developer's productivity, not to completely automate testing by guaranteeing 100% coverage. 

The tool is not meant for use by testers or system developers in production systems. It should only be used for development and debugging when working closely with developers. The generated tests are not bugs and should not be trusted for verification. They are meant to help design the tests to find the possible bugs.

The tool generates test cases but does not guarantee that it finds all possible paths that a bug might take, which is why it is recommended to pair Pex with a separate verification tool.
   
**Note: Always install the Pex and Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.Pex.Core NuGet packages before executing PEX files.**

Kind regards,
Shreyas

